<%!-- ============================================================
  Docs / Author your own action pack — self-maintained packs first;
  the public registry is curated.
============================================================ --%>

<div class="bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100">
  <.marketing_nav current_user={@current_user} current={:docs} />

  <section class="py-12 sm:py-16">
    <.docs_layout
      current="publishing-packs"
      updated="August 26, 2026"
      toc={[
        {"layout", "1. Lay out the pack directory"},
        {"manifest", "2. Write pack.yaml"},
        {"declare", "3. Declare each action"},
        {"install", "4. Validate, install & trust"},
        {"rollout", "5. Roll it out & maintain"},
        {"registry", "Keep it private, or propose it"}
      ]}
    >
      <.docs_header current="publishing-packs">
        <:dek>
          A pack is a directory of YAML you write and maintain yourself — there is nothing
          to compile or upload. Put it on the runners you control, trust it once, and your
          model can call it. Most packs stay private to your fleet; the public
          <.link navigate={~p"/packs"} class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200">
            registry
          </.link>
          is curated (<a
            href="#registry"
            class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
          >more below</a>).
        </:dek>
      </.docs_header>

      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="your host" copy_text="emisar pack validate ./my-pack"><span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> emisar pack validate ./my-pack
<span class="text-zinc-500">pack my-pack OK: 2 actions</span>
<span class="text-zinc-500">hash: sha256:e8edc3660cb2cf7a8f3fe0d9cea167d07565b8287f57bd089b4dd737eb0e3844</span>

<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> sudo emisar pack install ./my-pack
<span class="text-zinc-500">installed my-pack → /etc/emisar/packs/my-pack</span>

<span class="text-zinc-500">my-pack — My ops pack  (v0.1.0)</span>
<span class="text-zinc-500">  Short one-line summary shown on the runner + console.</span>

<span class="text-zinc-500">  Actions:   2  (2 low)</span>
<span class="text-zinc-500">  Requires:  linux · my-cli ✓</span>
<span class="text-zinc-500">  Hash:      sha256:e8edc3660cb2cf7a8f3fe0d9cea167d07565b8287f57bd089b4dd737eb0e3844</span>
<span class="text-zinc-500">  Docs:      https://github.acme.internal/sre/my-pack</span>

<span class="text-zinc-500">…</span>

<span class="text-zinc-500">  Verify</span>
<span class="text-zinc-500">    ok my.do_other_thing  5ms</span>

<span class="text-zinc-500">Reloaded the runner — it re-reads packs and re-advertises to the control plane.</span></.docs_code>

      <.agent_skill_cta
        class="mt-8"
        skill="author-pack"
        prompt="Write an emisar action pack for the tool I name, validate and test it, and get it trusted on my runner. Use the author-pack skill: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/AndrewDryga/emisar/main/skills/author-pack/SKILL.md"
      >
        The <code class="text-zinc-300">author-pack</code>
        skill walks your agent through writing, validating, testing, and trusting a pack of your own.
      </.agent_skill_cta>

      <.docs_h2 id="layout">1. Lay out the pack directory</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        A pack is a folder with a top-level <code>pack.yaml</code>
        manifest and one YAML file per action in <code>actions/</code>. An optional
        <code>scripts/</code>
        directory holds packaged shell scripts for <code>kind: script</code>
        actions. Keep the pack in your own Git repository.
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format>my-pack/
  pack.yaml                    # pack manifest (see step 2)
  actions/
    do_thing.yaml              # one declared action per file
    do_other_thing.yaml
  scripts/                     # only when you have script actions
    do_thing.sh</.docs_code>

      <.docs_h2 id="manifest">2. Write pack.yaml</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The manifest declares pack metadata and the action YAML files to load. <code>id</code>
        is the pack slug. It appears in the install path and your internal
        references.
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="yaml">schema_version: 1
id: my-pack
name: My ops pack
version: 0.1.0
description: Short one-line summary shown on the runner + console.
vendor: acme
homepage: https://github.acme.internal/sre/my-pack   # your repo; optional

requires:
  os: [linux]
  binaries: [my-cli]      # optional — a missing one fails at run time

setup:                    # optional — powers pack info, verify, and the install probe
  summary: >
    How the pack authenticates and what it targets.
  env:
    - name: MY_CLI_TOKEN
      description: Token my-cli reads. Allowlist it in execution.inherit_env too.
  verify: my.do_other_thing  # a no-argument, low-risk connectivity check

actions:
  - actions/do_thing.yaml
  - actions/do_other_thing.yaml</.docs_code>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The <code>setup</code>
        block is what <code>emisar pack info</code>
        prints and what <code>pack verify</code>
        and the install probe run: declare the env your tool reads, and name one low-risk
        read as <code>verify</code>.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="declare">3. Declare each action</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Create one YAML file for each action. The runner refuses anything outside the catalog.
        Each file defines the contract between your operations team and the LLM. Safety depends
        on tight argument validation, an accurate <code>side_effects</code>
        list, and the correct <code>risk</code>
        tier. See the
        <.link navigate={~p"/docs/action-packs"} class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200">
          Pack reference
        </.link>
        for all action fields.
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="yaml">schema_version: 1
id: my.do_thing
title: Do the thing
kind: exec
risk: low

description: >
  One-paragraph human description. Shown verbatim to the LLM —
  write it like a doc string, not a comment.

side_effects:
  - Reads /var/log/myapp/*.
  - Touches nothing.

args:
  - name: file
    type: path
    required: true
    validation:
      allowed_prefixes: ["/var/log/myapp/"]

execution:
  command:
    binary: cat              # bare name — resolved via PATH on the host
    argv: ["&#123;&#123; args.file &#125;&#125;"]
  timeout: 10s

output:
  parser: text
  max_stdout_bytes: 65536
  max_stderr_bytes: 8192

# Optional. Renders on the dispatch form so operators (and LLMs)
# see a real invocation before they fill in args.
examples:
  - title: Tail myapp's error log
    args:
      file: /var/log/myapp/error.log</.docs_code>

      <.docs_h2 id="install">4. Validate, install, and trust it</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Validate the pack locally — it runs the same checks as the runner — then install
        it. The install writes to the configured pack directory, reloads the runner
        without a restart or dropped runs, and ends by probing your <code>setup.verify</code>
        action, as the output at the top of this page shows:
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="shell"># on the runner host
emisar pack validate ./my-pack
sudo emisar pack install ./my-pack</.docs_code>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        You can validate anywhere: <code>pack validate</code>
        reads only the directory you point it at, and it does not need a config, a service,
        or an account. On a laptop — Linux or macOS — or in CI, install just the binary:
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="shell"><span class="text-zinc-500"># binary-only install — skips the service, needs no enrollment key</span>
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> curl -fsSL https://emisar.dev/install.sh | sudo bash -s -- --no-service
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> emisar pack validate ./my-pack

<span class="text-zinc-500"># in CI, pin the hash you will trust</span>
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> emisar pack validate ./my-pack --json | jq -r .hash</.docs_code>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Your pack is not in the published catalog, so it is not trusted automatically: it
        shows as <.status_badge status={:pending} /> on the console's
        <a
          href={~p"/app/packs"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Packs</a>
        page, and dispatch for it is refused until you review the content hash there and
        click <strong class="text-zinc-200">Trust</strong>. Its actions are then callable, gated by
        your policy, and that exact version is the only one authorized. Edit the pack and its
        hash changes, re-marking it pending until you re-trust — so what runs is always the
        files you reviewed.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-base text-zinc-400">
        Then test it end to end: open the runner from <a
          href={~p"/app/runners"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Runners</a>, check your actions under <strong class="text-zinc-200">Advertised actions</strong>, and
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Run</strong>
        one.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="rollout">5. Roll it out and maintain it</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        To put the pack on another runner, install it pinned to the hash you trusted — every
        host then runs the exact same files, and the install reloads the runner for you. Config
        management — Ansible, Chef, a base image — can drop the directory itself instead,
        then reload the runner.
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="shell">sudo emisar pack install ./my-pack --hash sha256:&lt;the hash you trusted&gt;</.docs_code>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        When you change the pack, increase its <code>version</code>. Validate and install it
        again. Add <code>--force</code>
        to replace an installed pack. Trust the new hash once in <a
          href={~p"/app/packs"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Packs</a>. Give it the same review as any production change.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Distributing many packs to a large fleet? Skip the copying — <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/pack-registry"}
          class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
        >host your own registry</.link>.
      </p>

      <%!-- The curated registry --%>
      <.docs_h2 id="registry">Keep it private, or propose it</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        We deliberately curate the public registry behind the <.link
          navigate={~p"/packs"}
          class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
        >
          Packs page
        </.link>. We accept only generic packs that many teams can use unchanged, such as Postgres,
        Cassandra, Docker, Linux core, and AWS packs. We reject one-off, environment-specific packs:
        they are hard to trust and maintain, and they bury the packs everyone can use.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Most packs must stay yours.</strong>
        A pack that hardcodes your service names, your hostnames, or your internal CLIs is more
        useful as a private pack. You also keep control of its lifecycle. Self-maintained is the default.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        A private pack can still serve a whole fleet —
        <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5">packctl</code>
        builds a registry you host yourself, and your packs never enter ours.
        <.link navigate={~p"/docs/pack-registry"} class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200">
          Host your own registry
        </.link>
        walks it end to end.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        If your pack is useful to many teams and has no environment-specific assumptions,
        publish it in its own repository and open an
        <.external_link
          href="https://github.com/andrewdryga/emisar/issues"
          class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
        >
          issue
        </.external_link>
        suggesting we include it. We review for:
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-3 space-y-3 pl-5 text-base text-zinc-400">
        <li class="list-disc">
          <strong class="text-zinc-200">Genuinely generic.</strong>
          Do not hardcode hosts, service names, or organization-specific paths.
        </li>
        <li class="list-disc">
          <strong class="text-zinc-200">Tight argument validation.</strong>
          Reject free-form shell strings. Use <code>allowed_prefixes</code>
          for paths, <code>validation.enum</code>
          for choices, and min/max for numbers.
        </li>
        <li class="list-disc">
          <strong class="text-zinc-200">Honest <code>side_effects</code>.</strong>
          List every file, network, and process side effect. The LLM reads this list.
        </li>
        <li class="list-disc">
          <strong class="text-zinc-200">Correct <code>risk</code> level.</strong>
          Assign <.docs_risk tier="high" /> or <.docs_risk tier="critical" />
          to destructive actions. Assign <.docs_risk tier="low" /> to reads.
        </li>
        <li class="list-disc">
          <strong class="text-zinc-200">Resolvable binaries.</strong>
          Declare required host binaries under <code>requires.binaries</code>.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <%!-- Next steps --%>
      <div class="mt-16 flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-3 border-t border-zinc-900 pt-8 text-sm">
        <.link navigate={~p"/docs/action-packs"} class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200">
          Pack reference
        </.link>
        <div class="flex items-center gap-4">
          <.link navigate={~p"/packs"} class="text-zinc-400 hover:text-zinc-200">
            Browse the registry
          </.link>
          <.external_link
            href="https://github.com/andrewdryga/emisar/tree/main/packs"
            class="text-zinc-400 hover:text-zinc-200"
          >
            Example packs on GitHub
          </.external_link>
        </div>
      </div>
    </.docs_layout>
  </section>

  <.marketing_footer />
</div>
